ents but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nThus scores:\nimplementability evidence sentence then 3\nacceptability evidence sentence then 4\napplicability evidence sentence then 4\neffectiveness evidence sentence then 3\noriginality evidence sentence then 2? maybe 3.\n\nLet's pick originality = 2 = interesting. Evidence: It applies familiar CI and replay testing to logs in a systematic way that is recognizable rather than surprising.\n\nParadigm_relatedness = 2.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably output format with fields.\n\nWe need for each subscale: <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Provide rating echoing id exactly.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2874\nimplementability_evidence: \"...\"\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: A CI job exercising log write-read cycles each sprint and quarterly replay tests can be built with existing engineering tools and staffing, requiring only modest process changes. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Ensuring logs remain readable and compliant aligns with legal requirements and industry norms for auditability and does not offend sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly addresses the Article 12 mandate to maintain technically functional automatic logs throughout a high-risk system's lifetime and traceability obligations. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Validating durability prevents late discovery of unreadable logs, which contributes materially to compliance but does not by itself create or operate the required logging capability. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Applying continuous-integration and time-travel replay to log schema longevity is an interesting engineering practice but is a recognizable extension of existing software quality testing rather than ingenious. Score 2 (or 3). Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds new tools and test routines for compliance logging while keep