        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate P2915.\n\nProposal: Shared Logging Middleware for All Pipelines. Single instrumentation SDK bolted onto existing MLOps and experiment-tracking stack that auto-captures Article 12(2) event categories... emit structured timestamped events into one central audit store maintained by infrastructure team, so no individual project re-derives its own logging logic. Amortises fixed compliance cost across all concurrent projects so individual R&D budgets not dented per system.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination 2=interesting 1=common mundane boring\n\nShared logging middleware for all pipelines. Is this common? In MLOps, shared instrumentation SDK is fairly common practice. Not ingenious surprising. Probably common, mundane. Might be interesting but not unusual. I'd say originality = 1? Or 2? The idea of centralized logging is common. Shared middleware to amortize compliance cost is common engineering. Likely common, mundane boring =1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to problem. 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nShared middleware is still treating compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still compliance as logging. That matches 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1? Standard compliance practice is to have logging. Centralized SDK maybe standard. Probably slightly paradigm stretching. I'll pick 2.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem) 3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial) 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem... 1 solves unrelated problem\n\nWill shared middleware meet logging mandate? It auto-captures Article 12(2) event categories for every high-risk system. That seems to directly solve logging mandate and amortize cost. Might be effective. Does it fully solve? Need also Article 12(3) specific requirements for point 1(a) Annex III: recording period of each use, reference database, input data match, identification of natural persons... The proposal mentions risk-trigger flags, reference-database calls, input-output pairs. Maybe covers but not all? Might be partial. Probably reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability? It captures categories. I'd say 4 = will solve stated problem. Could argue partial because need specific for biometric systems etc. But general.\n\nI'll give effectiveness 4? Let's see applicability separate. Effectiveness maybe 4.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. 4 solves identified problem directly related (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem) 3 solves implied problem... 2 may have some benefit within special situation...\n\nShared logging middleware directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes 3 some changes, or reasonably feasible measures 2 significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible 1 totally infeasible\n\nBolting SDK onto existing MLOps stack, central audit store maintained by infra team. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivial but doable. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Might require instrumentation of all pipelines, maybe significant change. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies that violate no norms... 3 somewhat uncommon... 2 offends sensibilities somewhat...1 radically violates...\n\nShared logging middleware is common, violates no norms. Acceptable. 4.\n\nNow need output format: Return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nOrder: originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nMake sure one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: \"A shared instrumentation SDK and central audit store for compliance logging is a common MLOps engineering pattern rather than an ingenious or surprising invention.\" Score 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"The proposal adds a reusable logging tool but still treats the EU AI Act mandate as a standard compliance/logging practice without changing how R&D relates to compliance.\" Score 2\n\neffectiveness_evidence: \"By auto-capturing Article 12(2) event categories for every high-risk system and centralizing them, it would meet the traceability and logging mandate if implemented.\" Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: \"The middleware is explicitly designed to satisfy Article 12 record-keeping for high-risk AI systems in the lab’s R&D context.\" Score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: \"Integrating an SDK into existing MLOps and experiment-tracking stacks and operating a central store requires moderate engineering effort but is reasonably feasible.\" Score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: \"Centralized compliance logging is a routine, norm-conforming practice that does not offend legal or business sensibilities.\" Score 4\n\nReturn JSON? Probably plain. The instruction says return rating echoing its id. Provide fields.\n\nLet's output as lines.\n\nMaybe format:\nid: P2915\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\n...\n\nProceed.",
