 1.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is compliant audit. Acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible\nTwo-week sprint with cross-functional team inspect live logs. Reasonably feasible, some changes. Could be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Maybe need resources but not huge. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Or 4 easy? Might be moderate cost. I'll give 3.\n\napplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal does gap analysis to confirm capture of four minimum data fields mandated by Article 12(3). Directly applies. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill gap analysis solve logging mandate? It identifies gaps but doesn't necessarily implement fix. It will contribute to solution, not fully solve. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness rating: reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). That is 3.\n\nAlternatively could be 4 if identifying gaps solves traceability? It doesn't actually implement logging. The proposal says confirm they already capture or flag where fall short. That's diagnostic not remediation. So partial.\n\nThus scores: paradigm_relatedness 1, originality 1, acceptability 4, implementability 3, applicability 4, effectiveness 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before number.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably output with id and fields.\n\nReturn rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\".\n\nWe'll output lines.\n\nLet's produce structured.\n\n",
