ated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Yes. Applicability 4.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Order listed: acceptability, implementability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nMake output clear.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably plain text. Might be bullet list.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\"...\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2784\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Reallocating internal engineering hours to meet a legal logging mandate is a routine, legally compliant business practice that offends no norms. Score 4.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Redirecting four existing FTEs via formal board descoping requires governance approval but remains feasible within frozen headcount. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Shifting resources from a lower-priority internal benchmark to compliance engineering is a common portfolio trade-off rather than ingenious. Score 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a cross-project logging platform group yet still treats compliance as separate logging work rather than integrating it into R&D. Score 2.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Providing dedicated staff will improve logging infrastructure resourcing but does not by itself guarantee Article 12 traceability and risk-logging adequacy. Score 3.\n\napplicability_evidence: Ring-fencing engineer time for logging directly addresses how the lab will meet the Article 12 record-keeping mandate. Score 4.\n\nOk.\n\n",
