g mandate? It adds overhead but doesn't guarantee logs are created per Article 12(3) specific requirements. It may facilitate compliance but not ensure recording of events, identification of natural persons etc. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial). Or 4? Hmm.\n\nThe mandate requires technically allow automatic recording. The proposal builds API over existing logging infrastructure. If logging infrastructure already exists, portal doesn't add logging. It just exposes. So it doesn't solve core logging requirement, it adds value on top. Effectiveness maybe 3.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences.\n\nImplementability_evidence: \"A lightweight API layer over existing logs and a client onboarding module require modest engineering and product work rather than rebuilding core R&D.\" Score 3.\n\nAcceptability_evidence: \"Providing read-only compliance dashboards to institutional clients aligns with transparency norms and does not violate legal or business sensibilities.\" Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"The proposal turns a regulatory logging obligation into a market-facing service offering, changing the relationship between compliance and the lab's commercial value proposition.\" Score 3.\n\nOriginality_evidence: \"Client-facing audit portals for AI logs are unusual in practice and show imagination in repositioning compliance as a differentiator.\" Score 3.\n\nApplicability_evidence: \"The dashboard directly addresses the mandate to make Article 12 logs traceable and usable by building on the required logging infrastructure.\" Score 4? Might be 3 solves implied problem? Hmm. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This does both. I'd keep 4.\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: \"If implemented, the portal would improve traceability and client confidence but does not by itself guarantee the automatic recording of all Article 12(3) events required for complianc