 paradigm preserving? No, it's not standard per project rebuild; it's shared service. That is new element. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nShared platform for compliance logging is common in software engineering. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting but not rare. I'd rate 1 or 2. The reference class: experienced evaluator expects AI R&D labs to give responses. Shared service layer is a fairly standard engineering response. Probably common, mundane, boring => 1.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce resources. Proposal meets logging mandate via shared service, reduces overhead. Should solve problem? It addresses meeting logging mandate efficiently. Seems effective. Probably 4? Will solve stated problem without regard for workability. If you could do it, it would solve main problem of meeting Article12 logging and keeping research budgets focused. I'd say 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation\n1 = intervention not stated or does not produce useful outcome\n\nIt directly applies: logging mandate. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon but do not offend\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates laws or sensibilities\n\nShared service internal platform is acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nReallocate 12% engineering headcount to four-person squad, define API in month one. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? It requires headcount reallocation. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3.\n\nLet's output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide evidence one sentence each.\n\nMake sure evidence sentence ends with period before score line? Probably separate lines.\n\nOutput JSON? Probably plain text. Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be key-value.\n\nProbably produce:\n\nid: P2777\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nCheck nuance: paradigm_relatedness 2 vs 3. \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\" Does shared service change relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations? It centralizes, but still compliance as separate plumbing. Might be slightly stretching. I'll keep 2.\n\nOriginality: common mundane boring =1. Could be interesting 2. Shared platform is standard DevOps practice. I'd say common. So 1.\n\nEffectiveness 4.\n\nApplicability 4.\n\nAcceptability 4.\n\nImplementability 3 (some changes). Could be 4 easy? Rea