ibute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Evidence: Audits can detect missing fields but do not create the logging capability required by Article 12(1)-(3). Score 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring. Quarterly self-audit and regulator-readiness drills is fairly common compliance practice. Probably common, mundane. Score 1? Could be 2 interesting? Self-audit is common. I'd say common, mundane boring => 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations... 3 paradigm stretching changes that relationship... 2 slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way of working unchanged.\n\nSelf-audit is standard compliance practice. So paradigm preserving =1. Evidence: The proposal treats Article 12 as standard logging practice to be monitored rather than rethinking R&D.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 easy low cost or non-radical changes. 3 some changes reasonably feasible. Self-audit quarterly with cross-functional teams sampling logs - requires staff time but feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 4 easy? Needs compliance staff, engineering time. Some changes. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. 4 common strategies violate no norms. Self-audit is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nNow format: Return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says give... Could be plain lines. Safer to output structured lines.\n\n