o which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Options:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nStandardised middleware is introducing new element tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's reusable component. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It changes relationship? Logging woven into runtime architecture before deployment. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools... ) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could argue 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nStandardised logging middleware for compliance is common practice. Many labs do SDK wrapper. Probably common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\nProposal is standard compliance. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nStandardised middleware is significant engineering effort but feasible. Platform engineering team design SDK... Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Might be moderate. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAlternatively could be 2 significant change expensive. Hmm. Standardized middleware across portfolio is significant change. But still feasible. I'd pick 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Instructions say return rating... Give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain lines.\n\nLet's produce structured output:\n\nid: P1786\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly implements automatic event logging for high-risk systems as mandated by Article 12(1)-(3). => score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the middleware would ensure every high-risk pipeline records the required logs before client deployment, solving the mandate. => 4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a reusable logging SDK and schema tool but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship. => 2\n\noriginality_evidence: Standardised logging middleware and SDK wrappers for regulatory compliance are common industry practice. =>1\n\nacceptability_evidence: Centralising mandated logging in software is legally required and socially unobjectionable. =>4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a reusable SDK and embedding it across pipelines requires platform engineering effort but is reasonably feasible. =>3\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
