blem statement says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So a research program is exploration, not implementation. That suggests it will not solve problem now. Might be 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem.\n\nLet's parse anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of the problem (it helps, but it is only a partial solution). 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work). 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nDoes research programme solve logging mandate? It could eventually lead to better logs, but does not meet immediate compliance. Could be considered partial solution: contributes to understanding how to log. I'd lean 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related... do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem\n3 = solves implied problem that is related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\nProposal does X = research programme, to get Y = scientific contribution, intellectual leadership. Is Y part of stated problem? Stated problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal adapts R&D to produce methodology, which is related but maybe implied problem of improving methodology. Could be 3 = solves implied problem that is related. Or 4 = solves identified problem directly related? Logging mandate is identified. Research on logging does relate.\n\nProbably applicability = 3? Let's see: do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Y = publishable research, open source reference implementation. That is not exactly meeting logging mandate, but helps meet it. Might be implied problem: need effective logging strategy. Could be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to wh