        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P2613.\n\nProposal: Gate Logging Depth to Product-Lifecycle Stage. Phased-embedding policy minimal event hooks for experimental/prototype, logging granularity escalates at internal 'intended purpose locked' milestone, exploiting proportionality language in Article 12(2). Lab's existing stage-gate review gains checkpoint where calibrate log fields and retention length to system's actual regulatory scope. Protects scientific velocity during weekly iteration while ensuring compliance architected into stack.\n\nWe need six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal gates logging depth to lifecycle stage, uses existing stage-gate. It changes relationship? It weaves logging into R&D lifecycle, not just bolt-on. That's change that relationship. Might be paradigm stretching (3). Does it introduce new elements AND changes relationship? It introduces phased-embedding policy, exploits proportionality. Changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - compliance is timed with lifecycle. That sounds like paradigm stretching not breaking. Breaking would address larger problem of what lab researches. I think 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious, imaginative, surprising\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nPhased logging based on lifecycle is plausible but not super rare? Might be interesting but not ingenious. Could be 2 or 3. I'd say 2 = interesting. Might be 3 unusual interesting. Experienced evaluator expects labs to propose phased approach? Possibly common. I think it's interesting but not unusual. Let's pick 2? Hmm.\n\nThe anchored: 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. This seems somewhat imaginative exploiting proportionality language. Could be 3. Let's choose 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nAdopt phased embedding policy, add checkpoint to stage-gate. Uses existing process. Seems reasonably feasible, some changes. That's 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon unusual strategies do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 radically violates laws...\n\nIs gating logging depth acceptable legally? Article 12 requires technically allow automatic recording over lifetime of system. Phased escalation may be okay if by deployment it's compliant. Proportionality language in 12(2). Acceptable? Probably somewhat uncommon but does not offend. Might be 3. Could be 4 common strategies violate no norms. Many labs do minimal logging early. I'd say 3.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 unreasonable unlikely to solve\n1 solves unrelated\n\nWill it solve problem of meeting logging mandate and/or adapt R&D? It ensures compliance by deployment while protecting velocity. Seems reasonable and will solve main problem. Could be 4. Without regard for workability, would solve. Yes.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation\n1 ...\n\nIt directly addresses logging mandate decision. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder: paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability, effectiveness, applicability\n\nProvide evidence one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal ties logging requirements to the lab’s internal stage-gate lifecycle, changing how R&D and compliance obligations interrelate rather than treating compliance as a separate bolt-on. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Exploiting Article 12(2) proportionality to escalate logging at “intended purpose locked” is an unusual, imaginative calibration of compliance depth. Score 3? Let's pick 3.\n\nimplementability_evidence: It reuses the existing stage-gate review and adds one checkpoint to calibrate log fields and retention, which is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Phased logging that meets full requirements by regulated deployment aligns with proportionality and does not offend legal or business norms. Score 4? Wait earlier said 3. Common strategies violate no norms -> maybe 4. Hmm. It is somewhat uncommon. I'd keep 3 to be safe. Let's pick 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: By architecting compliance into the stack at the locked-purpose milestone, it ensures logging mandates are met for high-risk deployment while preserving early-stage velocity. Score 4.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses how to meet Article 12’s logging mandate and adapt R&D workflow accordingly. Score 4.\n\nReturn JSON? Probably just list. Provide id and ratings.\n\nWe'll output as lines.\n\n",
