ial)\n2=unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem\n1=solves unrelated problem\n\nWill fundamental research solve logging mandate? Probably not solve main problem; it contributes maybe long term but not immediate compliance. It is unlikely to solve problem of meeting logging mandate now. Effectiveness maybe 2? Unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. Or 3 reasonable and will contribute? The stated problem includes adapt R&D accordingly, which this does. Might contribute.\n\nThe rating says approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. So research thread is exploration not immediate implementation. Effectiveness for solving mandate is low.\n\nProbably applicability 2 and effectiveness 2? Let's think: Applicability 2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem (do X which somehow relates). That fits.\n\nEffectiveness 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work). Research won't meet logging mandate quickly.\n\nAlternatively could be effectiveness 1 = solves unrelated problem. But it does relate to logs.\n\nI'd rate:\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal repurposes mandatory compliance logs as a scientific corpus for drift research, thereby changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations.\nScore 4\n\noriginality evidence: Examining information-theoretic content of Article 12 logs to map drift signatures is unusual and shows imagination beyond routine logging implementation.\nScore 3\n\nimplementability evidence: Running a longitudinal sandbox study and formalizing change-detection theory requires dedicated research staff and data infrastructure, making it reasonably feasible but not trivial.\nScore 3\n\nacceptability evidence: Publishing academic analysis of internally collected logs does not violate legal norms or business sensibilities.\nScore 4\n\napplicability evidence: The thread produces sc