y (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nBuild dedicated observability service on existing MLOps tooling that streams production logs... Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? It says on existing MLOps tooling. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Might need significant change. I'd give 3.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale (applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability) in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id. Provide lines.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P1028\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly ingests the Article 12(1) automatic event logs to satisfy traceability and post-market monitoring obligations. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, the pipeline would provide continuous risk correlation and alerting that fulfills Articles 12, 72 and 79 requirements. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds observability tooling but continues to treat compliance as a logging and monitoring practice rather than redefining R&D. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Building an always-on log ingestion and alerting pipeline on existing MLOps tooling is common industry practice for deployed AI. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Continuous monitoring of high-risk AI systems aligns with regulatory expectations and does not violate legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Leveraging existing MLOps infrastructure makes the service reasonably feasible though it requires integration, staffing, and SLA definition. Score 3.\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
