gineering. Common, mundane. Could be 1 or 2. Might be interesting but not unusual. Probably common, mundane, boring => 1.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\nProposal is standard compliance, no violation. So 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\nOne dedicated sprint team of four engineers for six-week effort to extend current observability stack. That's reasonably feasible. Could be 3? Easy to implement at low cost? Four engineers six weeks is moderate. Possibly 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 4 = easy? Hmm. I'd say 3. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal provides Article 12 compliant logging without per-team custom builds, amortises cost. Seems solves logging mandate. So 4? Will solve stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Yes. So 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nYes directly. So 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Could be lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1502\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal introduces a shared middleware tool but still treats Article 12 as a logging practice to be centralized