nterpretability contribution rather than mere compliance paperwork, which is unusual and shows imagination. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It weaves logging into the lab’s scientific work by making schema design a research output, changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Developing validated reference schemas with legal input will contribute to meeting Article 12 traceability requirements but does not by itself deploy logging in all systems. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Designing event-taxonomy schemas directly addresses the mandate to enable automatic recording of relevant events under Article 12(2). Score 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Open reference schemas developed with legal counsel and published academically violate no norms and align with regulatory expectations. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating formal schemas, validating across three prototypes and iterating with counsel requires dedicated researcher time but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
