ting is common. Maybe 2 = interesting but not rare? Could be 2 or 3. I'd say originality = 2: interesting management technique but not surprising.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost, non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\nThis seems fairly easy: re-score projects with coefficient. That's easy to implement at low cost. Maybe 4? Requires programme managers to attach multiplier before next quarter. That's some change but feasible. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Probably 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is internal budgeting, acceptable. 4.\n\nProvide output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale... give ... Probably list.\n\nI'll output JSON with id and fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1672\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability.\n\nProceed.",
