liance as logging practice.\n\nI think it's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces logging middleware component and template change, but still treats compliance as logging practice. So level 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedding auto-logging by default is fairly common compliance advice. Might be common/mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or maybe 2 interesting? I'd say common practice in regulated industry. So 1.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\nProposal does embed auto-logging as default to meet Article12 logging mandate. Directly applies. Solves identified problem. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nEmbedding auto-logging at architecture eliminates retrofitting, protects from technically-allow standard. Will solve logging mandate. Seems reasonable. So 4? It will solve logging mandate for new systems. Might not address existing systems. But still solves. I'd say 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nEmbedding logging is acceptable, no violation. Common. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\nMandating logging middleware in all new projects, updating templates, architecture review gate. That's significant change, requires engineering process change. Might be reasonably feasible but not easy low cost. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Maybe 2 = significant change expensive. I'd say some changes, reasonably feasible measures. So 3.\n\