        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nWe need rate proposal P2969.\n\nText: Institute High-Risk Classification Gate in R&D. mandatory compliance checkpoint embedded at project-proposal stage where every new system assessed against Annex I and III to determine whether it will be classified as high-risk. Prevents late-stage redesigns... compliance function co-chairs biweekly steering review... structured questionnaire mapped to Act's risk tiers and producing written classification memo... Early classification protects lab's reputation by ensuring no system reaches market without required logging and traceability features already architected in.\n\nRate subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is mandatory compliance checkpoint at project proposal stage, co-chair steering review. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance? It embeds compliance early. Is that paradigm stretching or slightly? Introduces new element tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice? It prevents late-stage redesigns, ensures logging architected in. Seems changes relationship: compliance co-chairs steering review, woven into science. Probably paradigm stretching: changes relationship (logging woven into science). Not breaking because doesn't change what lab researches. So 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: The gate embeds compliance assessment at project inception and co-chaired reviews, weaving logging obligations into R&D decision-making rather than treating them as post-hoc logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nClassification gate early is fairly common compliance practice. Might be common, mundane. Experienced evaluator would expect labs to do early classification? Possibly common. I'd say common, mundane, boring -> 1.\n\nEvidence: Early risk classification checkpoints are a standard compliance management practice and not surprising in regulated R&D settings.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal institutes classification gate to ensure logging features architected in early. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate by ensuring high-risk systems identified early so logging can be designed in. So applicability 4? Solves identified problem directly related. Yes.\n\nEvidence: Assessing high-risk status at proposal stage directly ensures logging and traceability are architected into systems subject to Article 12.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve? Classification gate prevents late redesigns, ensures logging features architected in. It helps meet mandate but does not itself implement logging. It contributes to solution but partial? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Early classification is necessary but not sufficient to actually log. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial.\n\nEvidence: Early classification reduces late redesigns and enables logging design, but it does not itself create the required logs or guarantee their correct implementation.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nCompliance checkpoint is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Embedding compliance review at project inception is a socially, legally and politically normal governance practice for regulated labs.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nNeeds compliance co-chair biweekly steering review, structured questionnaire, written memo. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Might require process change. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Creating a checkpoint, questionnaire and biweekly co-chaired review requires organizational change and staff time but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on separate lines.\n\nLet's output JSON? Prompt says return rating echo its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely key-value pairs.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P2969\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure sentences end with period.\n\nProvide exactly six subscales in order.\n\nProceed.",
