ability: solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Probably 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem. Could be 2? Might have some benefit within special situation... Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is to publish paper, not implement logging. Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. The proposal adapts R&D to produce knowledge about logging. That's relevant. I'd say applicability 3.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve the problem.\nWill it solve stated problem of meeting logging mandate? It contributes to solution but is only partial, not immediate compliance. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). So 3.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nUnusual, interesting; shows some imagination? Or not expressed before AND ingenious? Taxonomy based on information-theoretic criteria for log-event granularity seems unusual. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 4? Not expressed before AND ingenious. Possibly 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal forms cross-disciplinary working group to derive lower bounds, advances knowledge frontier, gives scientific basis for traceability design. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations - logging woven into science. Seems paradigm stretching. Could be 3. Is it paradigm breaking? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. It does advance knowledge frontier by giving field scientific basis. Might be paradigm breaking? Hmm definition: paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This seems like it changes relationship. Possibly 3 vs 4. It doesn't introduce new partners/offers. It is research paper. I think paradigm stretching = 3.\n\nLet's output JSON? Probably need fields.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably format like:\n\nid: P1739\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nProvide one sentence evidence for each.\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Publishing a taxonomy paper and forming an internal working group is a routine academic practice that does not violate legal, social, or business norms. acceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Validating information-theoretic lower bounds across three deployed prototypes over six months requires substantial engineering time, data access, and coordination, making it expensive and difficult but feasible. implementability:2\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal addresses the implied need to decide what counts as a 'relevant' log event under Article 12(2), which is central to meeting the logging mandate. applicability:3\n\neffectiveness_evidence: A principled taxonomy would inform future logging design and reduce legal ambiguity, but it does not itself deliver an operational logging system for immediate compliance. effectiveness:3\n\noriginality_evidence: Grounding log-event granularity in information theory and deriving completeness lower bounds is unusual and imaginative compared with typical ad-hoc engineering heuristics. originality:3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The work weaves complia